1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Meet the hardest, strongest material known to man.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Aluminum won't absorb that. Steel won't absorb that.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:10,000
But it's also one of the softest.

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
It's clean, dirty, brilliant, and black.

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:18,000
One teaspoon has the surface area of an acre.

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
It purifies air and water.

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
It powers the world while altering its climate.

8
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:27,000
It's the stuff of stars.

9
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:29,000
It's an enchanted element.

10
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:30,200
It's you and me.

11
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:34,700
Now, the untold story of carbon on Modern Marvels.

12
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:50,920
This specimen of high-tech muscle is known as a champ car.

13
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:58,980
The number 29 car competes around the world in races that draw as many as 300,000 fans.

14
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Believe it or not, a champ car's radical performance is helped by carbon.

15
00:01:06,001 --> 00:01:14,000
They are open wheel, single seat, open cockpit race cars, similar to Formula One.

16
00:01:14,001 --> 00:01:21,000
They're capable in the current aerodynamic configuration and horsepower of about 225 miles an hour.

17
00:01:21,001 --> 00:01:24,000
They make about 3,400 pounds of downforce.

18
00:01:24,001 --> 00:01:31,000
What that means is the car could drive upside down and hold itself to the ceiling because it makes so much downforce.

19
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
A champ car will go from zero to 100 in 2.6 seconds and then back to zero in the same amount of time.

20
00:01:40,001 --> 00:01:44,000
So basically you can go from zero to 100 to zero in about four seconds.

21
00:01:44,001 --> 00:01:45,560
four seconds.

22
00:01:45,561 --> 00:01:55,400
To hit speeds up to 225 miles per hour and take sharp road race turns at up to 165

23
00:01:55,401 --> 00:02:03,180
miles per hour, it helps to have a 750 horsepower engine, high-performance tires, an obsessive crew,

24
00:02:03,740 --> 00:02:10,040
and a really good driver. But it's the car's body that we're interested in.

25
00:02:10,041 --> 00:02:11,720
It's made of a super

26
00:02:11,721 --> 00:02:16,400
strong super light material that has revolutionized the world of motorsports

27
00:02:16,401 --> 00:02:19,720
carbon fiber

28
00:02:19,721 --> 00:02:23,540
carbon fiber is one of the latest incarnations of that simple element

29
00:02:23,541 --> 00:02:29,000
carbon that gives structure to all living things and helps other things go

30
00:02:29,001 --> 00:02:35,300
faster the main body section called the tub or monocoque the front and rear

31
00:02:35,301 --> 00:02:40,620
wings the body panels the driver's safety harness even certain engine

32
00:02:40,621 --> 00:02:44,740
components are made of the rather nondescript, even flimsy looking stuff.

33
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:48,640
Yeah, I don't know if you can see it, but I mean, pretty much the entire, I mean, the

34
00:02:48,641 --> 00:02:49,360
entire thing's carbon.

35
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:53,380
In fact, there's really not much I can see from in here that isn't carbon.

36
00:02:56,040 --> 00:03:00,380
Why are all Formula One, IndyCar, and Champ Car racers built with carbon fiber?

37
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:05,580
It's 10 times stronger than steel and three times lighter in most instances.

38
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:09,420
This is the engine cover from the current Champ Car.

39
00:03:09,421 --> 00:03:14,820
and as you can see it's very light and it's all because of carbon.

40
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:21,200
When the margin of victory can be a fraction of a second, every pound counts.

41
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:25,040
And the only thing more important than speed is safety.

42
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:29,300
From where I'm sitting for sure the reason for a carbon fiber would be its strength.

43
00:03:29,301 --> 00:03:34,080
The all one piece carbon fiber tub is really very safe

44
00:03:34,081 --> 00:03:37,500
and people walk away from some pretty nasty accidents.

45
00:03:37,501 --> 00:03:42,620
Driver Catherine Legg learned the value of carbon fiber firsthand.

46
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:50,980
During the 2006 Champ Car World Series, Legg's car smashed into the wall at 180 miles per hour.

47
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:56,380
Though the car splintered into dozens of pieces, Legg was unhurt.

48
00:03:57,220 --> 00:03:59,800
Her life was very likely saved by carbon fiber.

49
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:05,900
Some of these crashes at 200 miles an hour plus, there's no way a human would survive

50
00:04:05,901 --> 00:04:10,020
if the car wasn't absorbing a lot of the energy.

51
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:13,000
Aluminum won't absorb that. Steel won't absorb that.

52
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,440
Unlike metal, which crumples around the driver at the moment of failure,

53
00:04:17,980 --> 00:04:19,660
carbon fiber breaks away.

54
00:04:20,340 --> 00:04:24,500
It's very good for absorbing energy and not transferring it to the driver.

55
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:27,040
These cars are designed to break away,

56
00:04:27,041 --> 00:04:30,800
and when parts break off these cars, they take energy with them.

57
00:04:31,220 --> 00:04:34,700
So instead of that energy transferring into the driver and hurting the driver,

58
00:04:34,701 --> 00:04:37,640
the energy goes away with the part as it leaves.

59
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:42,480
Carbon fiber manufacture is as difficult as it is secretive.

60
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:48,440
But the Zoltec Corporation is opening the doors of its Abilene, Texas factory

61
00:04:48,441 --> 00:04:50,920
for a rare look inside its operation.

62
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:54,700
One of the strongest engineering materials in the world

63
00:04:54,701 --> 00:04:57,680
begins as a synthetic petroleum-derived fabric,

64
00:04:58,380 --> 00:05:00,180
not unlike nylon or polyester.

65
00:05:00,181 --> 00:05:06,300
We're standing here at the initial stage of the oxidation process, which typically

66
00:05:06,301 --> 00:05:11,760
stabilizes the man-made material or the polyacrylonitrile.

67
00:05:11,761 --> 00:05:14,260
We call it PAN for short.

68
00:05:14,261 --> 00:05:22,120
The pan material, as it's drawn into the first oxidation oven, begins to cook.

69
00:05:22,121 --> 00:05:29,760
And as it cooks, as it's drawn through this oven, the material starts to change color.

70
00:05:29,761 --> 00:05:34,660
What you're basically doing is driving off a lot of the other components and the more

71
00:05:34,661 --> 00:05:40,160
carbon you're left with, then the more black the material appears to be.

72
00:05:40,161 --> 00:05:45,080
The slow burn causes oxygen from the air to bond with the carbon atoms, which go from

73
00:05:45,081 --> 00:05:49,780
a linear chain to a stronger ladder-like formation.

74
00:05:49,781 --> 00:05:56,020
After the stabilization process, the fibers emerge as a very strong 68% carbon material

75
00:05:56,021 --> 00:05:57,020
known as pyrone.

76
00:05:57,021 --> 00:06:03,360
At this stage, this material can be used to create a break for an airliner.

77
00:06:04,980 --> 00:06:07,500
Although Pyron is tough enough to stop an airplane,

78
00:06:08,420 --> 00:06:12,020
turning it into 100% carbon fiber calls for a lot more heat.

79
00:06:12,660 --> 00:06:14,780
This is one of our high-temperature furnaces

80
00:06:14,781 --> 00:06:17,760
where we complete the carbonization of the fiber.

81
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:20,340
We have a total of five altogether,

82
00:06:20,341 --> 00:06:25,740
and in this furnace, we drive out the remains of the hydrogen and nitrogen

83
00:06:25,741 --> 00:06:28,300
to bring us up to a full carbon fiber.

84
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:31,660
This is really the most critical part of the process

85
00:06:31,661 --> 00:06:36,140
because the temperature that we use defines exactly the modulus

86
00:06:36,141 --> 00:06:39,640
or the stiffness of the fiber and also the tensile strength.

87
00:06:40,140 --> 00:06:43,320
So it's very, very important that we keep really accurate control

88
00:06:43,321 --> 00:06:45,640
of the furnaces at this stage of the process.

89
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:50,800
This time, the fibers are heated in the complete absence of oxygen

90
00:06:50,801 --> 00:06:52,340
so that they won't burn.

91
00:06:52,341 --> 00:06:59,340
Instead, the extreme heat forces the atoms to vibrate until the non-carbon atoms break

92
00:06:59,341 --> 00:07:01,580
away.

93
00:07:01,581 --> 00:07:07,380
Left behind are crystalline chains of carbon atoms.

94
00:07:07,381 --> 00:07:12,300
The carbonization process has now fused the carbon atoms into a very strong shape that

95
00:07:12,301 --> 00:07:15,780
will require great energy to break.

96
00:07:15,781 --> 00:07:20,900
The carbon fibers go through an electrochemical bath, which roughens the fiber surface and

97
00:07:20,901 --> 00:07:24,440
makes it more bondable for use in composites.

98
00:07:24,441 --> 00:07:28,280
Then, in a last step resembling a textile factory,

99
00:07:28,281 --> 00:07:31,460
the carbon fiber is wound onto bobbins.

100
00:07:31,461 --> 00:07:35,320
The fibers, each one about 1 tenth the width of a human hair,

101
00:07:35,321 --> 00:07:37,720
can be cut like any thread.

102
00:07:37,721 --> 00:07:41,180
But when bundled into a cord 50,000 fibers strong,

103
00:07:41,181 --> 00:07:43,200
they begin to flex their muscles.

104
00:07:43,201 --> 00:07:48,220
But how does carbon fiber go from this to this?

105
00:07:48,221 --> 00:07:50,720
First, the carbon fibers are woven into a fabric

106
00:07:50,721 --> 00:07:53,200
to provide multi-directional strength.

107
00:07:53,201 --> 00:07:59,200
That fabric is then draped over a mold of the vehicle itself.

108
00:07:59,201 --> 00:08:03,160
And they will apply resin to it and heat the whole thing up

109
00:08:03,161 --> 00:08:06,540
in an autoclave and cook it.

110
00:08:06,541 --> 00:08:08,460
The autoclave cooks the carbon fabric

111
00:08:08,461 --> 00:08:13,140
into a hard shell, with its shape determined by the mold.

112
00:08:13,141 --> 00:08:16,780
When you think about how it starts, to oversimplify it,

113
00:08:16,781 --> 00:08:19,400
it's a piece of rayon like what's in your shirt.

114
00:08:19,401 --> 00:08:27,520
And when you put it in an oven and you bake it and you do it right, you get something that can survive a 200-mile-an-hour accident and the guy gets up and walks away.

115
00:08:29,780 --> 00:08:32,440
Of course, carbon fiber isn't just for auto racing.

116
00:08:33,020 --> 00:08:39,760
It's making its way into high-performance consumer cars and the strong light blades on some of the world's largest wind turbines.

117
00:08:39,761 --> 00:08:48,040
But the most dramatic use for carbon fiber is on Boeing's new passenger jet, the 787

118
00:08:48,041 --> 00:08:51,820
Dreamliner, which debuts in 2008.

119
00:08:51,821 --> 00:08:56,480
The fuselage and wing structures are made almost entirely of a carbon fiber reinforced

120
00:08:56,481 --> 00:08:57,480
composite.

121
00:08:57,481 --> 00:09:03,160
The important thing about the carbon fiber is its strength and its light weight and its

122
00:09:03,161 --> 00:09:05,060
corrosion resistance.

123
00:09:05,061 --> 00:09:07,360
Those are the three key drivers.

124
00:09:07,361 --> 00:09:13,840
So the actual Dreamliner will be much lighter in weight than if it was made out of metal.

125
00:09:13,841 --> 00:09:17,720
And that's increasing the fuel efficiency.

126
00:09:17,721 --> 00:09:22,880
That will mean more affordable ticket prices, even as the cost of fuel goes ever upward.

127
00:09:22,881 --> 00:09:30,200
Back in the early 70s, Boeing estimated that every kilo you took off the weight of an aircraft

128
00:09:30,201 --> 00:09:35,780
would save something like $100,000 in fuel costs over the lifetime of the aircraft.

129
00:09:35,781 --> 00:09:38,780
In those days, oil was $3, $12 a barrel.

130
00:09:38,781 --> 00:09:43,160
Today, saving is potentially very much greater.

131
00:09:43,161 --> 00:09:48,820
While other airplanes must keep a cabin pressure equal to 8,000 feet above sea level, the Dreamliner

132
00:09:48,821 --> 00:09:53,560
with its stronger airframe will be able to maintain a much more comfortable 6,000 feet

133
00:09:53,561 --> 00:09:55,440
equivalent.

134
00:09:55,441 --> 00:09:59,860
And since carbon fiber will never corrode, airlines will be able to provide a more humid

135
00:09:59,861 --> 00:10:02,400
mix of air in the cabin.

136
00:10:02,401 --> 00:10:05,120
more dry eyes and throats at the end of a long flight.

137
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:11,960
It's taken 50 years for it to finally reach a new aircraft.

138
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:18,840
But the dream line will be the most fuel-efficient aircraft flying, and that's what carbon fibers

139
00:10:18,841 --> 00:10:19,541
was all about.

140
00:10:21,560 --> 00:10:25,080
Of course, carbon fiber is just a fraction of what carbon provides us.

141
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:30,960
Try to imagine a world without carbon, or even a champ car without carbon.

142
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:37,400
You can forget about the fancy hydrocarbon-derived paint job.

143
00:10:37,401 --> 00:10:40,400
You won't be able to stop without the carbon brake pads.

144
00:10:40,401 --> 00:10:44,400
But that's okay, because you can also remove the methanol fuel,

145
00:10:44,401 --> 00:10:48,400
the rubber tires, the car body itself,

146
00:10:48,401 --> 00:10:53,400
and last but not least, the carbon-based life form at the wheel.

147
00:10:53,401 --> 00:10:56,400
Just about everything that counts on this car,

148
00:10:56,401 --> 00:11:01,400
and in this world, is made at least partially of carbon.

149
00:11:01,401 --> 00:11:06,400
Carbon is crucial in forming the bonds that make up living things,

150
00:11:06,401 --> 00:11:10,400
and the stuff that certain living things like to make.

151
00:11:10,401 --> 00:11:15,400
Carbon is the most abundant element in the universe that forms solid structures.

152
00:11:15,401 --> 00:11:18,400
Of course, hydrogen and oxygen and helium are more abundant.

153
00:11:18,401 --> 00:11:20,400
Carbon forms structures.

154
00:11:20,401 --> 00:11:23,400
As a matter of fact, it's a very unique element.

155
00:11:23,401 --> 00:11:25,400
It's an enchanted element.

156
00:11:25,401 --> 00:11:31,560
The key lies in the configuration and size of the carbon atom.

157
00:11:33,560 --> 00:11:36,380
What makes carbon so versatile is two things.

158
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:41,400
Number one, the amount of electrons that it has in its outermost shell,

159
00:11:41,600 --> 00:11:44,640
which we refer to as the valence shell, is four.

160
00:11:45,320 --> 00:11:47,680
And it's also in the first row of the periodic table,

161
00:11:47,780 --> 00:11:49,220
which makes it a very small atom.

162
00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:54,960
The carbon atom's four valence electrons bond with larger elements,

163
00:11:54,961 --> 00:11:57,660
such as metals, as they give up electrons,

164
00:11:58,560 --> 00:12:01,260
and smaller elements, such as gases,

165
00:12:01,800 --> 00:12:03,280
as they try to gain electrons.

166
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:07,920
As a result, carbon forms an amazingly broad range of compounds.

167
00:12:08,860 --> 00:12:12,540
In fact, today there are probably over 10 million known carbon compounds.

168
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:18,720
The most stable carbon-to-carbon bond is found in diamond,

169
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:21,180
a crystalline lattice of pure carbon.

170
00:12:21,181 --> 00:12:26,360
Diamonds have extreme hardness because the carbons that exist within diamond

171
00:12:26,361 --> 00:12:30,020
have been compressed under extreme pressure over billions of years

172
00:12:30,021 --> 00:12:33,700
to form this interlocking network of tetrahedra.

173
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:37,700
And this interlocking network of bonds between carbons

174
00:12:37,701 --> 00:12:40,300
makes this extremely, extremely hard material.

175
00:12:40,460 --> 00:12:43,560
In fact, the hardest naturally occurring material known to man.

176
00:12:45,320 --> 00:12:47,780
Carbon, with its unique bonding capabilities,

177
00:12:48,080 --> 00:12:50,220
is also the key element of living things.

178
00:12:50,221 --> 00:12:53,220
Thus the phrase, carbon-based life forms.

179
00:12:53,221 --> 00:12:59,220
Carbon is an ideal chemical element to be just stable enough to make the stuff of life

180
00:12:59,221 --> 00:13:02,220
and the chemicals with which life does business,

181
00:13:02,221 --> 00:13:08,220
but just unstable enough that it doesn't cost too much energy to get that chemistry going.

182
00:13:09,220 --> 00:13:13,220
It's this ideal combination of stability and reactivity

183
00:13:13,221 --> 00:13:16,220
that makes carbon the backbone of all living systems.

184
00:13:16,221 --> 00:13:16,921
systems.

185
00:13:16,922 --> 00:13:23,220
The carbon providing the critical bonds, simple sugars, become complex molecules

186
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:31,000
like DNA. It's truly the element of life. Carbon-rich plant life of the prehistoric

187
00:13:32,680 --> 00:13:36,940
variety has proven one of our most powerful sources of energy.

188
00:13:36,941 --> 00:13:38,520
But can we find ways to

189
00:13:38,521 --> 00:13:45,520
burn it without increasing global warming? Astronomers have discovered a white dwarf

190
00:13:45,521 --> 00:13:52,920
known as BPM 37093, composed entirely of crystallized carbon, diamond.

191
00:13:53,720 --> 00:14:00,200
With roughly the same mass as our sun, the star forms a diamond of 10 billion trillion trillion carats.

192
00:14:01,300 --> 00:14:03,680
Carbon will return on Modern Marvels.

193
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:09,380
We now return to Carbon on Modern Marvels.

194
00:14:09,381 --> 00:14:19,780
The Antelope Valley Station, a 360-foot-high power plant in Beulah, North Dakota, more

195
00:14:19,781 --> 00:14:23,100
than anything else, is a really, really big boiler.

196
00:14:23,101 --> 00:14:28,560
Where we're standing is on the roof of Unit 1 of the Antelope Valley Station.

197
00:14:28,561 --> 00:14:32,860
In the background is the Unit 2 of the Antelope Valley Station.

198
00:14:32,861 --> 00:14:40,260
As designed in the late 70s, this was the largest boiler in physical size anywhere in the world.

199
00:14:40,800 --> 00:14:43,740
We're physically standing on the tallest building in North Dakota.

200
00:14:45,160 --> 00:14:49,560
North Dakota's tallest building is here because of what was left here a long time ago.

201
00:14:50,580 --> 00:14:55,040
Carbon. Lots of it. Trapped in a bounty of prehistoric life.

202
00:14:55,041 --> 00:15:03,120
Between 50 and 250 million years ago, North Dakota and this region was a huge inland sea.

203
00:15:03,980 --> 00:15:07,160
There was a tremendous amount of vegetation that grew up from that.

204
00:15:08,100 --> 00:15:13,500
And over time, that vegetation grew and then died and got covered and compressed.

205
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:19,520
Over millions of years, layers of sediment compacted the vegetation into peat.

206
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:24,640
Eventually, the pressure, combined with heat rising from the Earth's mantle,

207
00:15:25,040 --> 00:15:30,020
broke down the peat into a black, carbon-rich substance we know as coal.

208
00:15:31,040 --> 00:15:34,980
The Antelope Valley Station gets its coal from the nearby Freedom Mine,

209
00:15:35,420 --> 00:15:37,320
the largest coal mine in North Dakota.

210
00:15:38,100 --> 00:15:41,340
16,000 tons each day are crushed into a fine powder,

211
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:44,220
ready to enter the pair of 33-story boilers,

212
00:15:44,380 --> 00:15:46,740
where coal does what it does best, burn.

213
00:15:48,720 --> 00:15:51,600
As it burns, it releases energy in the form of heat,

214
00:15:52,160 --> 00:15:54,800
held in the tightly formed prehistoric carbon bonds.

215
00:15:55,040 --> 00:16:02,700
The 2,600 degree heat is used to boil 14,000 gallons of water, about half a swimming pool

216
00:16:02,701 --> 00:16:07,260
per minute, into high pressure, 1,000 degree steam.

217
00:16:07,261 --> 00:16:13,180
The steam is forced into turbines, which spin fast enough to turn this 160 ton shaft, visible

218
00:16:13,181 --> 00:16:19,280
only at this small gap, 3,600 times per minute, generating half a million horsepower.

219
00:16:19,281 --> 00:16:26,280
Everything on this facility is here to make sure that the shaft rotates at 36 RPM.

220
00:16:26,281 --> 00:16:34,280
The electric current generated from the power plant's two turbine decks is enough to power 360,000 homes.

221
00:16:34,281 --> 00:16:43,280
For all the talk about America's addiction to oil, the nation's energy infrastructure is truly built on the bedrock of coal.

222
00:16:43,281 --> 00:16:48,740
The U.S. has by far the world's largest deposits of coal.

223
00:16:49,360 --> 00:16:55,700
Thanks to the swamps and tropical forests that covered much of eastern North America about 300 million years ago.

224
00:16:57,140 --> 00:17:02,600
And the vast inland sea that covered what are now the Great Plains 100 million years ago.

225
00:17:03,380 --> 00:17:06,660
It's not only domestic, but it's a plentiful fuel.

226
00:17:07,120 --> 00:17:09,660
There's hundreds of years available for use.

227
00:17:09,661 --> 00:17:17,620
In fact, half of the country's electricity is produced from coal, so it's a very important fuel for us just from that aspect.

228
00:17:19,080 --> 00:17:23,520
In China, 82% of the electricity comes from the burning of coal.

229
00:17:24,500 --> 00:17:26,700
In India, it's 70%.

230
00:17:26,701 --> 00:17:30,560
Coal also fueled the Industrial Revolution.

231
00:17:31,820 --> 00:17:35,300
Large coal deposits in England and America powered the age of steam.

232
00:17:35,301 --> 00:17:39,560
Locomotives, steamships, and factories.

233
00:17:42,140 --> 00:17:46,920
Steelmakers learned how to turn coal into a cleaner-burning, pure carbon fuel known as coal,

234
00:17:47,660 --> 00:17:52,720
which supplies the tremendous heat and carbon atoms needed to turn iron ore into steel.

235
00:17:55,880 --> 00:18:00,060
But our planet has paid a heavy price for a century and a half of coal usage.

236
00:18:01,080 --> 00:18:04,800
Coal causes more pollution per unit of energy than any other fuel.

237
00:18:05,300 --> 00:18:10,560
Its more dangerous particulates include mercury, arsenic, and sulfur dioxide.

238
00:18:11,260 --> 00:18:13,820
And as the most carbon-rich of the fossil fuels,

239
00:18:14,460 --> 00:18:18,240
coal is the number one emitter of the global warming gas carbon dioxide,

240
00:18:19,180 --> 00:18:22,020
producing a third of all human-caused CO2 emissions.

241
00:18:23,880 --> 00:18:27,760
When you burn something, you're combining it with oxygen.

242
00:18:28,660 --> 00:18:32,520
So when you burn a material that's made of carbon,

243
00:18:32,521 --> 00:18:35,780
what happens is you rapidly combine it with oxygen.

244
00:18:36,420 --> 00:18:40,140
So one thing that happens is you get carbon and oxygen together.

245
00:18:42,140 --> 00:18:47,280
Reducing coal's carbon dioxide emissions will be a daunting task, but not impossible.

246
00:18:49,240 --> 00:18:54,220
Next door to the Antelope Valley Power Plant is the Great Plains Sin Fuels plant,

247
00:18:54,720 --> 00:18:58,140
which uses an innovative technique known as coal gasification.

248
00:18:58,560 --> 00:19:01,480
Coal gasification is a very unique process.

249
00:19:02,260 --> 00:19:07,300
The gasification plant here is the only plant of its kind in the United States.

250
00:19:08,720 --> 00:19:14,240
Inside the 14 gasifiers, high-pressure tanks each weighing 165 tons,

251
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:19,580
a precise combination of heat, pressure, oxygen and steam

252
00:19:19,581 --> 00:19:23,120
causes the molecular structure of the coal to break apart.

253
00:19:24,240 --> 00:19:28,160
The hydrogen and carbon form pure methane, or natural gas,

254
00:19:28,161 --> 00:19:33,920
while the principal product methane is piped off to be used as a heating fuel

255
00:19:33,921 --> 00:19:40,960
other compounds such as ammonium sulfate are captured by smokestack scrubbers and

256
00:19:40,961 --> 00:19:48,640
used to make fertilizer i think one of the unique aspects of this plant is that this is a chemist's

257
00:19:48,641 --> 00:19:54,000
dream unlike coal combustion plants which release a more jumbled mix of emissions

258
00:19:54,001 --> 00:20:01,000
the Sinfuels plant can separate, capture and compress its carbon dioxide emissions.

259
00:20:01,001 --> 00:20:08,000
The CO2 waste stream is sent through a pipeline of these depleted oil fields in Canada.

260
00:20:08,001 --> 00:20:13,000
Here the CO2 is injected many hundreds of feet below the surface

261
00:20:13,001 --> 00:20:17,000
into porous rock formations which trap the CO2 permanently.

262
00:20:17,001 --> 00:20:21,000
It's called carbon dioxide sequestration

263
00:20:21,001 --> 00:20:26,000
and this expensive technology is just getting started.

264
00:20:26,001 --> 00:20:30,000
Meanwhile, the world's CO2 emissions are growing every year,

265
00:20:30,001 --> 00:20:34,000
due in large part to the increasing use of coal.

266
00:20:34,001 --> 00:20:38,000
But another carbon-based energy form is also on the rise.

267
00:20:38,001 --> 00:20:42,000
Instead of prehistoric plant energy, it harnesses living plants

268
00:20:42,001 --> 00:20:47,000
and their remarkable ability to remove carbon dioxide from the air.

269
00:20:47,001 --> 00:20:52,600
Well, plants are the ultimate recyclers of carbon for us in the biosphere.

270
00:20:52,601 --> 00:20:58,880
Without plants, no animal life would be possible, because what plants do is take carbon dioxide

271
00:20:58,881 --> 00:21:05,680
from the atmosphere and they use the energy in sunlight to fix that carbon dioxide, make

272
00:21:05,681 --> 00:21:09,620
it into part of the plant's structure.

273
00:21:09,621 --> 00:21:14,360
Most of a plant's structure is composed of cellulose and hemicellulose, complex chains

274
00:21:14,361 --> 00:21:19,500
of sugars that form the strong fibrous tissue that holds a plant upright.

275
00:21:19,501 --> 00:21:24,120
For three decades, a handful of scientists have labored to turn those cellulosic sugars

276
00:21:24,121 --> 00:21:30,240
into ethanol, a biofuel that's powering more vehicles every year.

277
00:21:30,241 --> 00:21:37,260
Unlike corn ethanol, which ferments only the starchy grain into alcohol, cellulosic ethanol

278
00:21:37,261 --> 00:21:40,740
would use the entire plant, virtually any plant.

279
00:21:40,741 --> 00:21:44,020
The good part is that there's an awful lot of cellulose out there in the world,

280
00:21:44,420 --> 00:21:46,160
hundreds of billions of tons, literally.

281
00:21:46,680 --> 00:21:50,940
What's bad about it is that Mother Nature designed those plant cell walls,

282
00:21:51,060 --> 00:21:52,300
designed the cellulose molecule,

283
00:21:52,840 --> 00:21:56,820
so it's not very easy to take the cellulose apart into individual sugar molecules.

284
00:21:58,020 --> 00:22:00,120
But thanks to advances in technology,

285
00:22:00,660 --> 00:22:02,840
especially in the enzymes and other compounds

286
00:22:02,841 --> 00:22:05,320
that break down the cellulose into fermentable sugars,

287
00:22:06,240 --> 00:22:09,660
scientists and entrepreneurs have figured out how to replace a fossil fuel,

288
00:22:09,661 --> 00:22:14,220
oil with a living fuel, cellulosic ethanol.

289
00:22:15,540 --> 00:22:18,880
There are many environmental advantages to cellulosic ethanol.

290
00:22:19,920 --> 00:22:25,020
Unlike corn, cellulose-heavy crops like switchgrass thrive on marginal lands

291
00:22:25,021 --> 00:22:28,060
and require little or no pesticides and fertilizer,

292
00:22:28,620 --> 00:22:30,720
which are generally derived from fossil fuels.

293
00:22:31,860 --> 00:22:32,630
The result?

294
00:22:32,631 --> 00:22:39,000
Cellulosic ethanol crops have a net energy return 10 times greater than that of corn grain.

295
00:22:39,660 --> 00:22:41,740
Well, switchgrass is native to the United States.

296
00:22:41,880 --> 00:22:45,500
It was one of the grasses, in fact, it was the dominant grass in the grasslands

297
00:22:45,501 --> 00:22:47,740
that covered the Great Plains before the settlers arrived.

298
00:22:48,260 --> 00:22:52,240
It's a C4 perennial grass, means it's very good at fixing carbon dioxide.

299
00:22:53,820 --> 00:22:58,420
Being a perennial grass, its roots continue to sequester carbon dioxide in the soil,

300
00:22:59,020 --> 00:23:00,760
even after its stalks have been harvested.

301
00:23:02,280 --> 00:23:04,300
Now it's a question of economics.

302
00:23:05,260 --> 00:23:09,880
As scientists and industry continue to knock down the price of the enzymes and other technology,

303
00:23:10,360 --> 00:23:16,860
they predict that within 10 to 15 years, they'll be producing cellulosic ethanol for under a dollar a gallon.

304
00:23:17,420 --> 00:23:21,140
If we do this right, if we pay proper attention to how we build the systems,

305
00:23:21,141 --> 00:23:27,700
we can have fuels that are inexpensive, that provide environmental benefits, lots of environmental benefits.

306
00:23:27,701 --> 00:23:36,340
For better and for worse, humanity has changed the landscape and the atmosphere with carbon-based fuels.

307
00:23:37,220 --> 00:23:41,300
But to really make our mark, we reach for a different carbon tool.

308
00:23:42,780 --> 00:23:53,641
Planting 300,000 new trees and allowing them a full life would offset the carbon dioxide emissions of one 500-megawatt coal-fired power plant, operating for a little

309
00:23:53,642 --> 00:23:54,760
more than a week.

310
00:23:54,761 --> 00:23:57,180
Carbon will return on Modern Marvels.

311
00:23:57,700 --> 00:24:04,700
We now return to Carbon on Modern Marbles.

312
00:24:08,580 --> 00:24:11,860
Take the pure carbon atoms inside a diamond.

313
00:24:11,861 --> 00:24:17,100
Change their rigid lattice into a stack of loosely interlocking sheets.

314
00:24:17,101 --> 00:24:19,980
And you transform the hardest known material

315
00:24:19,981 --> 00:24:24,380
into one of the softest minerals on the planet, graphite.

316
00:24:24,381 --> 00:24:30,580
Here's graphite in its solid form, and you can see that the top of this is very flat,

317
00:24:30,920 --> 00:24:34,560
representing where the top sheet would be, so to speak, of the graphite layer.

318
00:24:34,980 --> 00:24:40,560
And all the layers that are beneath it are very loosely held together by these intermolecular forces.

319
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:44,500
And the intermolecular forces that are easily broken allow these sheets to slide.

320
00:24:46,020 --> 00:24:49,080
Anyone who's used graphite to open a sticky lock

321
00:24:49,081 --> 00:24:53,260
knows that the mineral's slippery structure makes it a good industrial lubricant.

322
00:24:54,380 --> 00:24:57,460
Even more so because it can withstand extremely high temperatures.

323
00:24:58,500 --> 00:25:03,300
The steel industry, they'll use it to lubricate doors as its doors have to slide up and down.

324
00:25:03,760 --> 00:25:06,660
They use it for crucibles that you would fire items in.

325
00:25:07,580 --> 00:25:08,940
It's in brake shoes lining.

326
00:25:09,380 --> 00:25:11,100
Bearings are actually made out of it.

327
00:25:11,320 --> 00:25:13,340
On the bottom of the space shuttles that comes in,

328
00:25:13,400 --> 00:25:17,060
those black tiles that hold the heat back as the space shuttle comes back in,

329
00:25:17,340 --> 00:25:20,420
that's actually made out of a graphite product as well.

330
00:25:20,421 --> 00:25:27,420
But graphite has made its name literally with a wonderful ability to leave a mark.

331
00:25:27,421 --> 00:25:34,700
At General Pencil Company's factory in Jersey City, New Jersey, a dedicated group of pencil

332
00:25:34,701 --> 00:25:40,060
makers turn graphite powder and blocks of cedar into the world's most reliable writing

333
00:25:40,061 --> 00:25:41,060
instrument.

334
00:25:41,061 --> 00:25:48,120
We are still the oldest pencil manufacturer in the USA, still family owned and operated

335
00:25:48,121 --> 00:25:50,180
by the original founding family.

336
00:25:50,181 --> 00:25:53,900
And we're still in this brick building 100 years later.

337
00:25:57,360 --> 00:26:00,400
Making a pencil begins with a mixture of graphite,

338
00:26:00,401 --> 00:26:06,420
carbon powder derived from coal, and a smaller amount of clay.

339
00:26:06,421 --> 00:26:08,020
Increasing the percentage of graphite

340
00:26:08,021 --> 00:26:11,800
makes for a softer, darker mark, while more clay

341
00:26:11,801 --> 00:26:13,160
makes a harder, lighter mark.

342
00:26:13,161 --> 00:26:20,160
A 750 pound mix of graphite, carbon, clay and water are fed into this ball mill filled

343
00:26:22,120 --> 00:26:23,496
with river stones.

344
00:26:23,497 --> 00:26:28,620
The ball mill churns the ingredients for 24 hours until they form a

345
00:26:28,621 --> 00:26:30,506
smooth homogenous slurry.

346
00:26:30,507 --> 00:26:35,560
General Pencil pumps the slurry through filter presses which screen

347
00:26:35,561 --> 00:26:40,602
out the water and form the mix into manageable sections known as cakes.

348
00:26:40,603 --> 00:26:41,880
The cakes are left

349
00:26:41,881 --> 00:26:43,800
to dry for 48 hours.

350
00:26:43,801 --> 00:26:47,000
We want to dry it real slow so that the dry evenly

351
00:26:47,001 --> 00:26:48,840
in the outer surface doesn't crust.

352
00:26:48,841 --> 00:26:51,500
If it should crust, that's going to be a scratchy spot

353
00:26:51,501 --> 00:26:52,560
in our lead later.

354
00:26:54,880 --> 00:26:57,560
Pencil makers and the general public still use the term

355
00:26:57,561 --> 00:27:00,920
lead to describe the pencil's graphite writing core,

356
00:27:00,921 --> 00:27:03,200
even though pencils have never contained lead.

357
00:27:04,980 --> 00:27:07,480
The misnomer comes from the fact that ancient Romans,

358
00:27:07,481 --> 00:27:10,920
Greeks, and Egyptians wrote with a tool called a stylus,

359
00:27:10,921 --> 00:27:16,200
pensillis in Latin, which was often made of lead.

360
00:27:16,201 --> 00:27:23,840
The modern-day pencil traces its origins to the discovery of graphite in 1564.

361
00:27:23,841 --> 00:27:28,720
What perfecting the pencil took three centuries of innovations, many of which are still used

362
00:27:28,721 --> 00:27:33,220
by general pencil.

363
00:27:33,221 --> 00:27:39,160
After the carbon cakes are pulverized to a very fine powder and recombined with water,

364
00:27:39,161 --> 00:27:43,700
the mixture is ready to be extruded into the pencil-shaped cords, or legs.

365
00:27:46,320 --> 00:27:48,840
He's packing it in our packing ram,

366
00:27:49,220 --> 00:27:52,760
and what he's having to do is get all of the air out of there,

367
00:27:52,840 --> 00:27:55,000
just like you would make a clay figure in school.

368
00:27:55,120 --> 00:27:58,900
If you have air in it, it's going to explode when we go to fire these.

369
00:28:01,700 --> 00:28:03,880
Okay, John's loaded up our cylinder,

370
00:28:04,160 --> 00:28:07,640
and now for the first time, the actual mix itself

371
00:28:07,641 --> 00:28:09,820
is going to take on the shape of a pencil lead.

372
00:28:11,040 --> 00:28:13,700
The lead is going to come out of the die here

373
00:28:13,701 --> 00:28:15,680
and pass through our cutter wheel.

374
00:28:16,680 --> 00:28:18,120
And as the lead comes through,

375
00:28:18,300 --> 00:28:20,500
these blades will cut it to length,

376
00:28:20,760 --> 00:28:23,060
and that die is what's actually going to give us

377
00:28:23,061 --> 00:28:25,140
the shape of the pencil lead itself.

378
00:28:32,540 --> 00:28:36,020
Once the pencil cores are extruded out and dried,

379
00:28:36,021 --> 00:28:39,820
to place into the crucibles which we're actually going to put into our furnace.

380
00:28:40,520 --> 00:28:47,900
On top of the crucible, we pour a packing graphite to make sure that the oxygen doesn't get down to leads,

381
00:28:48,040 --> 00:28:50,600
the cores themselves, to burn out that carbon.

382
00:28:54,340 --> 00:28:57,560
While the 1,800-degree furnace stiffens the leads,

383
00:28:59,600 --> 00:29:03,280
over in the woodworking area, the pencil shell begins to take shape.

384
00:29:03,281 --> 00:29:08,060
First, a saw cuts eight grooves into each slat of cedar.

385
00:29:08,061 --> 00:29:13,220
We use the cedar because of its straight grain and cedar allows for smooth sharpening

386
00:29:13,221 --> 00:29:14,220
as well.

387
00:29:14,221 --> 00:29:21,560
And of course, everybody loves the smell of a freshly sharpened cedar pencil.

388
00:29:21,561 --> 00:29:32,120
The graphite cores are glued into one slat and covered with another, forming a sandwich.

389
00:29:32,121 --> 00:29:35,740
In stride, the slants are cut into round or hexagonal pencils.

390
00:29:36,660 --> 00:29:38,920
We make a hex pencil and a round pencil.

391
00:29:39,480 --> 00:29:44,220
Traditionally, a hex pencil was made so it simply would not roll off the desk of a child.

392
00:29:47,160 --> 00:29:49,580
The pencils are then sanded and painted,

393
00:29:50,920 --> 00:29:55,740
fitted with a brass eraser holder called a ferrule, in case you were wondering,

394
00:29:56,620 --> 00:29:58,540
and the erasers themselves.

395
00:29:58,541 --> 00:30:05,700
They're also stamped with letters or numbers that indicate the pencil's degree of hardness

396
00:30:05,701 --> 00:30:08,860
versus blackness, the HB scale.

397
00:30:08,861 --> 00:30:16,180
With one being the softest, two being soft and leaving a nice dark mark, and three being

398
00:30:16,181 --> 00:30:20,020
slightly harder, and four being extra hard.

399
00:30:20,021 --> 00:30:25,780
The number two pencil also happens to leave a mark that's easily read by optical scanners.

400
00:30:25,781 --> 00:30:30,780
That's why beginning in the 1960s, every student has had to bring a pair to take a standardized

401
00:30:30,781 --> 00:30:31,780
test.

402
00:30:31,781 --> 00:30:38,140
The number 2 is but one point along a wide scale of pencil options.

403
00:30:38,141 --> 00:30:42,660
To get a fine light mark, an engineer might use the number 5H.

404
00:30:42,661 --> 00:30:48,040
An artist seeking bold black lines would reach for an 8B.

405
00:30:48,041 --> 00:30:52,340
One of the benefits that a pencil has over a pen is just by the pressure of your hand

406
00:30:52,341 --> 00:30:56,500
You can change the texture, the darkness, the lightness, the tone, the imprint,

407
00:30:56,840 --> 00:30:59,200
where a pen just isn't going to be able to do that for you.

408
00:31:01,000 --> 00:31:03,340
Graphite works by leaving itself behind.

409
00:31:04,160 --> 00:31:07,940
But another form of carbon is unparalleled in soaking up other stuff.

410
00:31:08,340 --> 00:31:13,260
And it's keeping your air, water, and even your feet fresh and clean.

411
00:31:15,080 --> 00:31:18,240
China, which has the world's largest graphite deposits,

412
00:31:18,800 --> 00:31:21,440
has become the world's most prolific pencil manufacturer.

413
00:31:21,441 --> 00:31:25,020
turning out about 9 billion pencils per year.

414
00:31:26,380 --> 00:31:28,740
Carbon will return on Modern Marvels.

415
00:31:30,320 --> 00:31:33,540
We now return to Carbon on Modern Marvels.

416
00:31:35,460 --> 00:31:38,340
In the hills near the Kentucky-West Virginia border,

417
00:31:39,060 --> 00:31:44,500
the Calgon Carbon Corporation makes a black, granular, utterly unremarkable substance

418
00:31:44,501 --> 00:31:46,760
that may have already saved your life.

419
00:31:47,460 --> 00:31:50,420
This substance is the key component of gas masks,

420
00:31:50,421 --> 00:32:00,300
poison antidotes, air purifiers, water filters large and small, and literally hundreds of other products.

421
00:32:01,280 --> 00:32:03,060
It's known as activated carbon.

422
00:32:04,220 --> 00:32:07,080
Carbon naturally has strong bonding capabilities.

423
00:32:07,960 --> 00:32:13,140
In activated carbon, this ability is greatly extended by the addition of microscopic pores.

424
00:32:13,141 --> 00:32:21,060
The internal pores give activated carbon tremendous surface area and make it the most adsorbent material known to man.

425
00:32:21,400 --> 00:32:26,060
One teaspoon of activated carbon has the surface area of an acre,

426
00:32:26,680 --> 00:32:31,920
and one gram of activated carbon has the surface area of a football field.

427
00:32:32,980 --> 00:32:39,020
With its ability to adsorb dangerous chemicals or undesirable tastes, odors, and colors,

428
00:32:39,021 --> 00:32:43,480
activated carbon may be the most useful thing you'll never see.

429
00:32:45,300 --> 00:32:51,800
I was trying to think about how many times a day a person might come in contact with activated carbon,

430
00:32:51,940 --> 00:32:55,540
and I think I got exhausted after I got through, like, breakfast.

431
00:32:55,960 --> 00:32:57,000
You take a shower.

432
00:32:57,580 --> 00:33:02,500
The shampoo and the soaps probably have chemicals in them that have been purified with activated carbon.

433
00:33:02,920 --> 00:33:07,020
There are components of the toothpaste that have been purified with activated carbon.

434
00:33:07,021 --> 00:33:14,260
To have a cup of coffee, well, if it's decaffeinated coffee, activated carbon has been used in the decaffeination process.

435
00:33:14,700 --> 00:33:21,680
You may have purified the water that you're putting with the coffee, and the sweetener that you use has been purified with activated carbon.

436
00:33:24,940 --> 00:33:27,060
How does carbon become activated?

437
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:36,260
At the Calgon plant, the world's largest granular activated carbon plant, it begins with crushed granules of bituminous coal.

438
00:33:36,261 --> 00:33:38,200
A hard, low ash variety.

439
00:33:40,560 --> 00:33:43,900
The coal granules go into a rotating 120-foot baker.

440
00:33:45,240 --> 00:33:50,140
The primary purpose of the baker is to take a sized coal particle

441
00:33:50,141 --> 00:33:52,680
and to heat it up in a very controlled fashion

442
00:33:52,681 --> 00:33:55,980
such that the volatile matter is removed from the coal particle.

443
00:33:56,560 --> 00:34:00,660
And the total heating process takes the material from ambient temperature

444
00:34:00,661 --> 00:34:03,500
to roughly 800 to 900 degrees F.

445
00:34:06,260 --> 00:34:13,260
The baking process leaves behind pure carbon, which is fed into the activating tower.

446
00:34:13,261 --> 00:34:17,260
The 90-foot tower is a kind of complex furnace.

447
00:34:17,261 --> 00:34:21,260
Precise inputs of steam and oxygen feed a roaring blaze.

448
00:34:21,261 --> 00:34:27,260
The material is heated up to roughly 18 to 1900 degrees aft in the presence of steam.

449
00:34:27,261 --> 00:34:31,260
What we're looking at is the bottom hearth of the activator.

450
00:34:31,261 --> 00:34:38,660
The steam penetrates the carbon particles, burning millions of tiny pores, creating channels

451
00:34:38,661 --> 00:34:44,260
that will be used to absorb undesirable chemicals.

452
00:34:44,261 --> 00:34:51,320
The carbon now officially activated is cooled, screened and loaded into containers ranging

453
00:34:51,321 --> 00:34:54,820
from 1,000-pound bags to 20-ton tankers.

454
00:34:54,821 --> 00:35:01,260
There are dozens of different sized activated carbon products designed specifically for

455
00:35:01,261 --> 00:35:06,960
different types of impurities and whether they're air or waterborne.

456
00:35:06,961 --> 00:35:09,980
But the principle is pretty much the same.

457
00:35:09,981 --> 00:35:15,380
Methylene blue is one of the colored solutions we use to measure pore size in carbon.

458
00:35:15,381 --> 00:35:19,900
So it's a fairly common test on activated charcoal or activated carbon.

459
00:35:19,901 --> 00:35:24,700
Okay, so again you can see the carbon bed stops about here.

460
00:35:24,701 --> 00:35:30,880
blue is above it, so we'll just drain the colored solution through the carbon bed, and

461
00:35:30,881 --> 00:35:37,620
as it goes through the carbon, removing the blue colored molecules from the water.

462
00:35:37,621 --> 00:35:41,480
Activated carbon's ability to remove color makes it indispensable to food and beverage

463
00:35:41,481 --> 00:35:50,620
manufacturers, who use it to make beer clear and sugar white as the driven snow.

464
00:35:50,621 --> 00:35:55,380
But activated carbon's most important job is absorbing dangerous chemicals from our

465
00:35:55,381 --> 00:35:58,800
water supply as it passes through a water treatment plant.

466
00:35:58,801 --> 00:36:04,700
And as the water actually goes through a granular bed of carbon, the carbon is pulling out those

467
00:36:04,701 --> 00:36:09,840
organic molecules, whether it's molecules that cause taste and odor in the water or

468
00:36:09,841 --> 00:36:16,700
maybe even some potentially harmful molecules that are pesticides or herbicides.

469
00:36:16,701 --> 00:36:22,600
Since activated carbon does not kill off microorganisms, treatment facilities use chlorine to sanitize

470
00:36:22,601 --> 00:36:26,020
the drinking water supply.

471
00:36:26,021 --> 00:36:31,080
Carbon filter technology has trickled down to home water filters, which use activated

472
00:36:31,081 --> 00:36:35,540
carbon to remove further impurities and the taste of the chlorine.

473
00:36:35,541 --> 00:36:40,620
Sure it's important to have pure air and water, but what you may ask can activated

474
00:36:40,621 --> 00:36:44,980
carbon do for my smelly feet?

475
00:36:44,981 --> 00:36:46,794
Thanks to the work of Dr.

476
00:36:46,795 --> 00:36:51,220
Herbert Lapidus, the inventor of odor eaters, you're in luck.

477
00:36:51,221 --> 00:36:55,620
Odor eaters were the first product to tackle the odor causing bacteria that thrive in the

478
00:36:55,621 --> 00:36:59,500
sweaty, confined environment of a shoe.

479
00:36:59,501 --> 00:37:01,200
Just think of your shoe.

480
00:37:01,201 --> 00:37:07,340
You have provided the bacteria with a magnificent growth media.

481
00:37:07,341 --> 00:37:11,100
And worst, it's trapped in your shoe.

482
00:37:11,101 --> 00:37:13,820
There's no place to go.

483
00:37:13,821 --> 00:37:18,580
To absorb the bacteria, the Lapidus process relies on activated charcoal derived from

484
00:37:18,581 --> 00:37:20,480
a surprising source.

485
00:37:20,481 --> 00:37:26,220
We use activated charcoal derived from coconut shells.

486
00:37:26,221 --> 00:37:32,320
It's a higher quality and more expensive, but I was interested in small amounts, so

487
00:37:32,321 --> 00:37:40,260
I was looking for the very finest, and NASA had come away with the coconut shell base

488
00:37:40,261 --> 00:37:42,420
as the best activated charcoal you could get

489
00:37:42,421 --> 00:37:44,940
for clearing air in space capsules.

490
00:37:45,840 --> 00:37:48,660
But the breakthrough was in Odor Eater's delivery system.

491
00:37:50,400 --> 00:37:54,380
You can't just take charcoal, dust it in the shoe,

492
00:37:54,381 --> 00:37:55,980
and let people walk around with it,

493
00:37:55,981 --> 00:37:58,180
so I knew that couldn't work.

494
00:37:58,181 --> 00:38:01,980
What you have in Odor Eater's is activated charcoal

495
00:38:01,981 --> 00:38:06,980
embedded in this breathing, porous, latex foam.

496
00:38:06,981 --> 00:38:10,980
Every time you walk, you express the air.

497
00:38:10,981 --> 00:38:12,980
It's like a bellows effect.

498
00:38:12,981 --> 00:38:14,980
You walk on it and the air moves,

499
00:38:14,981 --> 00:38:18,980
and so the air with the odor moves through the latex,

500
00:38:18,981 --> 00:38:22,980
which has the activated charcoal embedded in the latex,

501
00:38:22,981 --> 00:38:26,980
and the activated charcoal picks up those odor molecules.

502
00:38:26,981 --> 00:38:31,980
The design worked, and for millions of people suffering from extreme foot odor,

503
00:38:31,981 --> 00:38:35,980
and those around them, the results have been anything but trivial.

504
00:38:35,981 --> 00:38:40,580
Our marriages were breaking up because they couldn't take off their shoes at home.

505
00:38:40,860 --> 00:38:43,500
The wife wouldn't wash their socks.

506
00:38:45,060 --> 00:38:47,440
Once again, it's carbon to the rescue.

507
00:38:48,840 --> 00:38:51,760
New forms of carbon are protecting valuable space vehicles

508
00:38:51,761 --> 00:38:55,180
and may someday form a ladder to the stars.

509
00:38:57,740 --> 00:38:59,520
In the early 20th century,

510
00:39:00,240 --> 00:39:02,960
coconut shells were the most common source of activated carbon,

511
00:39:03,560 --> 00:39:05,120
including use in gas masks.

512
00:39:05,980 --> 00:39:14,200
During World War II, Japanese control of the coconut supply in the Pacific forced American-activated carbon makers to switch to coal.

513
00:39:15,540 --> 00:39:17,940
Carbon will return on Modern Marvels.

514
00:39:19,560 --> 00:39:22,980
We now return to Carbon on Modern Marvels.

515
00:39:25,340 --> 00:39:29,720
This truly space-age technology is known as an aerogel.

516
00:39:29,721 --> 00:39:35,780
Nearly weightless, with tremendous surface area and heat shielding capabilities,

517
00:39:37,120 --> 00:39:40,660
aerogel has already been used to capture particles from a speeding comet

518
00:39:40,661 --> 00:39:45,560
and to protect valuable equipment from the intense radiation of space.

519
00:39:46,660 --> 00:39:49,080
And scientists are just starting to explore this stuff.

520
00:39:50,140 --> 00:39:52,560
An aerogel is similar to an aerosol.

521
00:39:53,460 --> 00:39:56,420
It's a dispersion of liquid or solid particles in a gas.

522
00:39:56,980 --> 00:39:59,820
But in an aerogel, the particles have bonded together.

523
00:40:00,360 --> 00:40:06,120
So that's why aerogel, even though they're like 99% air, they're still considered a solid.

524
00:40:07,220 --> 00:40:11,860
A handful of aerogel has the same surface area as about two tennis courts.

525
00:40:13,020 --> 00:40:17,720
And yet a wafer of aerogel weighing only half an ounce can support the weight of a brick.

526
00:40:17,721 --> 00:40:25,880
NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory put these remarkable qualities to work during the 2004

527
00:40:25,881 --> 00:40:30,717
Stardust mission, which sought to understand the composition of comets.

528
00:40:30,718 --> 00:40:32,420
Stardust used a collector

529
00:40:32,421 --> 00:40:37,723
made of porous silica aerogel to capture particles of the comet VILD2.

530
00:40:37,724 --> 00:40:39,920
So as these particles hit the

531
00:40:39,921 --> 00:40:46,700
aerogel at a very high velocity, six kilometers per second, they basically just crushed the aerogel

532
00:40:46,701 --> 00:40:51,980
network and in doing so created a track into the aerogel. So it was basically

533
00:40:51,981 --> 00:40:56,240
slowing down as it embedded itself in the aerogel and eventually came to a stop.

534
00:40:56,241 --> 00:41:02,140
The next generation of aerogels is made with carbon.

535
00:41:02,141 --> 00:41:07,080
Carbon aerogel begins as a clear cocktail of organic chemicals which are combined

536
00:41:07,081 --> 00:41:12,140
with water. They all react to actually form a gel and this is actually what the

537
00:41:12,141 --> 00:41:13,721
gel looks like here.

538
00:41:13,722 --> 00:41:18,780
The gel then goes into a high-pressure oven called an autoclave,

539
00:41:19,220 --> 00:41:22,580
which cooks off everything but pure carbon.

540
00:41:22,581 --> 00:41:25,940
Carbonara gel delivers the same porosity as

541
00:41:25,941 --> 00:41:31,700
silica, but brings better heat insulation. A wafer of carbonara gel weighing next to

542
00:41:31,701 --> 00:41:33,367
nothing can do this.

543
00:41:33,368 --> 00:41:39,700
Such lightweight insulation makes carbonara gel an ideal material to protect

544
00:41:39,701 --> 00:41:43,300
the Mars rover vehicles from the harsh radiation of space.

545
00:41:43,301 --> 00:41:48,580
The same type of material is also going to be used on the Mars Science Laboratory rover,

546
00:41:48,581 --> 00:41:53,960
which will be going up in a few years.

547
00:41:53,961 --> 00:41:59,460
Scientists plan to tap another of carbon's virtues, strength to revolutionize space travel.

548
00:41:59,461 --> 00:42:04,040
Currently, there's only one way to escape the Earth's gravity.

549
00:42:04,041 --> 00:42:09,600
But rocket-powered liftoffs are expensive, not to mention dangerous.

550
00:42:09,601 --> 00:42:14,260
Today scientists are inching closer to an outlandish sounding alternative known as the

551
00:42:14,261 --> 00:42:17,440
space elevator.

552
00:42:17,441 --> 00:42:23,680
It would consist of a cable 62,000 miles in length, tethered to Earth, with a counterweight

553
00:42:23,681 --> 00:42:32,320
circling the Earth just outside of geosynchronous orbit, higher than 22,240 miles.

554
00:42:32,321 --> 00:42:36,160
The centripetal force of the anchored tether working against the counterweight's orbital

555
00:42:36,161 --> 00:42:41,860
speed would keep enough tension on the cable to allow cargo to be lifted along its length

556
00:42:41,861 --> 00:42:45,720
and into space.

557
00:42:45,721 --> 00:42:48,320
Space elevator is a fascinating idea.

558
00:42:48,321 --> 00:42:50,740
Why hasn't it been done before?

559
00:42:50,741 --> 00:42:57,220
Because with current materials, the weight of the cable would make the cable collapse

560
00:42:57,221 --> 00:42:59,780
to the earth.

561
00:42:59,781 --> 00:43:04,140
But there is one material that could work, carbon nanotubes.

562
00:43:04,141 --> 00:43:08,280
Carbon nanotubes have a unique combination of properties.

563
00:43:08,281 --> 00:43:09,720
They are tough.

564
00:43:09,721 --> 00:43:11,540
They are strong.

565
00:43:11,541 --> 00:43:17,280
They are 100 times stronger than steel at 1 sixth of the weight.

566
00:43:17,281 --> 00:43:21,800
Carbon nanotubes are nanometer-sized particles of graphite in which the bonding structure

567
00:43:21,801 --> 00:43:26,280
has been radically strengthened.

568
00:43:26,281 --> 00:43:33,300
If this represents one layer of graphite, this is a carbon nanotube.

569
00:43:33,301 --> 00:43:38,460
The super-strong particles are assembled on the nanoscale over and over again to form

570
00:43:38,461 --> 00:43:41,460
life-sized structures.

571
00:43:41,461 --> 00:43:46,020
Suddenly the idea of a space elevator is not so outlandish.

572
00:43:46,021 --> 00:43:53,620
With nanotubes being, again, 100 times stronger than steel and at one-sixth of the weight,

573
00:43:53,621 --> 00:44:00,460
this appears to be feasible and NASA is really investing in this idea.

574
00:44:00,461 --> 00:44:03,760
And that's just one application for carbon nanotubes.

575
00:44:03,761 --> 00:44:07,700
In the years to come, you can expect to see them greatly extending the battery life of

576
00:44:07,701 --> 00:44:15,100
hand-held electronic devices, acting as molecular scale semiconductors in smaller, more powerful

577
00:44:15,101 --> 00:44:22,780
computers, and providing a super strong biocompatible material in replacement joints for us carbon-based

578
00:44:22,781 --> 00:44:26,620
lifeforms.

579
00:44:26,621 --> 00:44:34,220
is the little element that could and does form the lightest, strongest, hardest, softest,

580
00:44:34,221 --> 00:44:42,020
most absorbent, most useful, most dangerous and most interesting things on this planet.

